 The area of a parallelogram is given by the formula: \[
\text{Area} = \text{side}_1 \times \text{side}_2 \times \sin(\theta)
\] where \(\theta\) is the angle between the sides. In this case, the sides are \(s\) and \(2s\), and the angle is 45 degrees. The area is given as \(8\sqrt{2}\). So we can write: \[
s \times 2s \times \sin(45^\circ) = 8\sqrt{2}
\] Since \(\sin(45^\circ) = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\), we can substitute this in: \[
s \times 2s \times \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = 8\sqrt{2}
\] Simplifying the left side: \[
s^2 \sqrt{2} = 8\sqrt{2}
\] Dividing both sides by \(\sqrt{2}\): \[
s^2 = 8
\] Taking the square root of both sides: \[
s = \sqrt{8} = 2\sqrt{2}
\] <end think> <answer> $2\sqrt{2}$ </answer>
